<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="page3-poker" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>One-Card Two-Round Poker</title>

  <p>
    We're gonna start analyzing a version of poker with game theory, so maybe
    by the end of the course you'll want to hit up the casinos! We will study
    a much simpler version than standard poker (<q>one-card two-round
    poker</q>).
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-5-4-setup">
    <title>Section 5.4, The Setup</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 5.4 up through both sets of bullet points.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      So you are stopping after the three options for Player 1 when he has
      the high card:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>bb</alert>: bet. If Player 2 bets back, bet again.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>bf</alert>: bet. If Player 2 bets back, fold.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>f</alert>: fold.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      The line you stop at is <q>If Player 1 is dealt the low card...</q> At
      this point you will have a sense of the setup and strategies. We will
      clarify a few points before moving on.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Note that the last three bullet points are Player 1's options when he has
    the high card. He has those same three options when he has the low card.
  </p>

  <p>
    One technicality: the option f is really two options lumped together.
    Strictly, a strategy says what Player 1 does at every decision point, so
    we'd have <q>fold, then bet the second time</q> and <q>fold, then fold
    the second time.</q> But once he folds the game is over, so the book just
    calls both f. (It does the same with Player 2's f.)
  </p>

  <p>
    Think about what this means for the total number of (pure) strategies,
    counting f as one option: three options with the high card and three
    with the low card. Remember the multiplication rule we saw earlier in
    the course!
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-poker-nine-strategies">
    <statement>
      <p>
        How many pure strategies does Player 1 have?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>3</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            bb, bf, and f are options for <em>one</em> card. A strategy has to
            say what Player 1 does with both cards.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p><m>9</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. A strategy picks one of <m>3</m> options for the high card
            and one of <m>3</m> for the low card, so <m>3 \times 3 = 9</m>.
            If we didn't lump the two fold options together as f, each card
            would have <m>4</m> options and we'd get <m>4 \times 4 = 16</m>.
            The extra strategies only differ in choices that never come up,
            so the book doesn't count them separately.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>6</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            A strategy is a <em>pair</em> of choices, one for each card, so we
            multiply. Think of outfits: <m>3</m> shirts and <m>3</m> pants
            make <m>9</m> outfits, not <m>6</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    When you read on, you're going to see only three strategies for Player 1
    in the tables. This is because the book throws out every strategy that
    doesn't bet at every opportunity with the high card. That leaves three
    strategies, and the book names each one by its low card option only:
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <alert>bb</alert>: bb if high, bb if low. Always bet, whatever the
          card.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <alert>bf</alert>: bb if high, bf if low. Always bet at first. With
          the low card, fold if Player 2 bets back.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <alert>f</alert>: bb if high, f if low. Fold right away with the low
          card.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <p>
    This is why, when you get to the next table (for when the card dealt is
    high), all of the rows are the same! In all three of these strategies,
    Player 1 always bets if he has the high card. The names bb, bf, and f only
    say what he does when the card is low.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-5-4-solving">
    <title>Section 5.4, Finding the Equilibrium</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Keep reading through the derivation of the Nash equilibrium
      <m>(q_1, q_2, q_3) = \left(\frac{8}{15}, \frac{2}{15},
      \frac{1}{3}\right)</m>.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      So at this point you will have seen the expected payoff tables, the
      system of equations for finding the Nash equilibrium, and the solutions
      <m>(p_1, p_2, p_3) = \left(\frac{1}{5}, \frac{2}{5},
      \frac{2}{5}\right)</m> for Player 1 and <m>(q_1, q_2, q_3) =
      \left(\frac{8}{15}, \frac{2}{15}, \frac{1}{3}\right)</m> for Player 2.
      A few things to watch for:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            In the high card table, all three rows are the same. That makes
            sense now: all three strategies play bb with the high card.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The expected payoff matrix shows up twice. The second copy is the
            same matrix with the probabilities (the <m>p</m>'s and
            <m>q</m>'s) in the margins.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The book skips the cases with two active strategies and says it
            will come back to them in Section 5.5.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Now let's make sure you understand a few important ideas from this
    reading!
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-poker-why-equal">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Why does the book set
        <m>\pi_2(\sigma_1, bb) = \pi_2(\sigma_1, bf) = \pi_2(\sigma_1, f)</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Condition (2) of Theorem 5.2.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Condition (2) compares active strategies to inactive ones, and
            it's an inequality. Here every strategy is active, and we want
            equalities.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Player 1 is mixing, so Player 1 must be indifferent.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Wrong player. These are Player 2's payoffs, so this is about
            Player 2 being indifferent among <em>his</em> strategies.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Condition (1) of Theorem 5.2. All of Player 2's pure strategies are
            active, so they have to earn him the same payoff against
            <m>\sigma_1</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Same idea as Tennis, just with three strategies instead
            of two.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="check-poker-sum-one">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Where does the equation <m>p_1 + p_2 + p_3 = 1</m> come from?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            The <m>p_i</m> are the probabilities of Player 1's three
            strategies, and he has to play one of them. This means they sum
            to <m>1</m> (a 100 percent chance of playing one of these
            strategies).
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Probabilities always add up to <m>1</m>. The two
            indifference equations aren't enough to pin down three unknowns,
            so we need this one too.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            It's a third equation from Player 2 being indifferent.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Setting three things equal only gives two independent equations.
            This third one has nothing to do with payoffs. It's just a fact
            about probabilities.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            It comes from the game being zero-sum.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Zero-sum is about the players' <em>payoffs</em> adding to
            <m>0</m>. This equation is about Player 1's probabilities.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    So from the three equations (two from Theorem 5.2, and one from the
    probabilities summing to <m>1</m>), the book finds
    <m>(p_1, p_2, p_3) = \left(\frac{1}{5}, \frac{2}{5},
    \frac{2}{5}\right)</m>. Before reading on, let's think about what this
    actually means!!
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-poker-interpret-p2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        What does <m>p_2 = \frac{2}{5}</m> mean?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Player 2 bets back and then folds <m>\frac{2}{5}</m> of the time.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The <m>p</m>'s belong to Player 1. Player 2's probabilities are
            the <m>q</m>'s.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\frac{2}{5}</m> of the time, Player 1 uses bf: bb if high, bf
            if low.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Remember bf is a full strategy (the name refers to what
            Player 1 does when dealt the low card). With the high card he
            still bets at every opportunity.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            With the high card, Player 1 bets and then folds
            <m>\frac{2}{5}</m> of the time.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            He never folds with the high card! The name bf only describes
            what he does with the low card.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="check-poker-expected-payoff">
    <statement>
      <p>
        How would you compute Player 1's expected payoff
        <m>\pi_1(\sigma_1, \sigma_2)</m>? (Don't actually compute it.)
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Add up the three diagonal cells, where both players use the
            strategy with the same name.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Every cell can happen, since each player picks his strategy
            independently. Plus the names mean different things for the two
            players, so the diagonal isn't special.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Average the nine payoffs.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            That treats every cell as equally likely. Each cell happens with
            probability <m>p_i q_j</m>, so weight by that instead.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Take a weighted sum over all nine cells: each cell's Player 1
            payoff times <m>p_i q_j</m>, the probability of landing in that
            cell.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. That's equation (5.2), same as Tennis but with nine cells
            instead of four. Reading across the rows of the payoff matrix:
            <md>
              <mrow>\pi_1(\sigma_1, \sigma_2) = \amp\ p_1 q_1 \cdot 0 + p_1 q_2 \cdot 4 + p_1 q_3 \cdot 2</mrow>
              <mrow>\amp + p_2 q_1 \cdot 1 + p_2 q_2 \cdot 0 + p_2 q_3 \cdot 2</mrow>
              <mrow>\amp + p_3 q_1 \cdot 2 + p_3 q_2 \cdot 1 + p_3 q_3 \cdot 0</mrow>
            </md>
            Since this is an equilibrium, Theorem 5.3 also gives a shortcut.
            You'll see both in the reading.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-5-4-rest">
    <title>Section 5.4, The Rest</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Finish reading Section 5.4.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      The rest is interpreting the results, including the two things you just
      reasoned out: what the probabilities mean as plans and how to compute
      the expected payoff.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-5-4" iframe="question-form.html?section=5.4" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
